So I went in to talk to Mutti. And I said, why is [? Isa ?] crying so? And said, well, I guess it's because you have to go to Stuttgart tomorrow. I said, what's in Stuttgart? Why do I have to go to Stuttgart? That's a city in southern Germany.

And she said, you have to go there by train in order to get your visa. What's a visa? Well, you're going to go to America. I am? Yes. And Elsa-- that was the youngest sister of my mother's, the one that I was so fond of-- worked for the Jewish welfare in Frankfurt.

And in March of that year, a letter had come across her desk from an organization in the United States, the Felix Warburg Committee, which proposed to bring Jewish children to the United States because of the threat from Hitler's regime.

But there were qualifications. The children had to be under 12. They had to be good students, and because of the Hitler situation, would be deprived of higher education. And the parents had to be on welfare. None of the three categories applied to us, either to my parents or to myself.

But my aunt asked my parents, shall I try to get Lotte on there? And they told her, go ahead and try. It's the only means of our getting somebody from the family out. We had no relatives in this country who had given-- who could have given affidavits.

My father would never have been admitted into this country because of his handicap. And my mother would not have left him alone. And my brother had already gone to France to try to get a work permit there, which in the end, he was unsuccessful in obtaining and had to come back to Germany.

And so they told her to go ahead and try. And I guess, in September, then, that's when they found out that she was successful, and that the order had come through, and that I needed to go to Stuttgart to get those papers. And I-- I don't know how come, but I went alone.

And then the parents told me, too, you'll be going there for a year. That was the-- they were not making that up, I don't believe. I think it was their firm belief that this was just going to be for a year. At that time, perhaps still, people were thinking that in a year, Hitler would be out of office. And for a year, OK, I agreed to go without much of a fight. Because I wanted to stay with my Zionist friends and go to Palestine.

So to make a long story short, I got the papers. And on the 27th of November, I left Frankfurt and sailed from Bremen to come to the United States. My mother was the chaperone.

There was another child from Frankfurt that was on the same transport, quite a bit younger than myself. She was in the categories that were prescribed. And Mother escorted us to Hamburg, where we picked up, see, probably, about another 12 youngsters from all over Germany.

And then Mother took us to Bremen to get on the ship there and turned us over to some authorities on the ship. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] we sailed for the United States.

INT: How did you-- you don't know, then, how your mother or-- or your parents arranged-- if you didn't fit the categories, how did you?

LM: I don't know what strings my aunt pulled. I have no idea. But somehow, I was accepted. And I was not the only one that was over the age limit and all that. Because one of the boys who joined us from Berlin, he was as old as I was, if not even a little bit older. So I imagine that they just had to have some sort of limits. And then they could deviate from those limits and make exceptions.

INT: How did you feel about it when your parents told-- your mother told you you'd be leaving?

LM: Well, at first, I was upset because I thought at that time it would be forever. But then the parents assured me it would be for one year. And that pacified me, because I was going to come back to my group of friends at the youth organization. But--

INT: Were you still friendly at this time with your best girlfriend, Fredel?

LM: Oh, yes, very much so.

INT: Was she going with you at all of this?

LM: No, no.

INT: Did you know any other children at the time that were going with you?

LM: No. There was just one other girl from Frankfurt-- one other person from Frankfurt that was going. That was this younger girl that my mother escorted along with me. And I didn't know her beforehand. I don't know if my mother knew the family or not. I think there was only a mother living. So it was a single-parent family.

INT: What did you take with you?

LM: Clothing, one photo album, one blank book to use as a diary. I'd never done a diary before, but Mother thought that maybe I would like to jot down a few things. After a while, I did start doing it too. And my camera [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and probably a few little books that I had been enjoying reading, and I think Fredel gave me a little book as a remembrance. But that's was about-- about it.

In retrospect, I'm quite sure that my mother expected me to come back too, because otherwise, she probably would have given me some jewelry to take out of Germany. I had one-- two necklaces. One was a necklace that my father had given my mother when they got engaged. And another one was a silver rope necklace that an uncle gave me. And those were the only two-- and my watch-- the only two pieces of jewelry that I brought with me.

And I'm sure that Mother and Father, if they would have had any inkling what was coming, that they would have given me more things to take along. That-- because in 1934, the restrictions were not as tight as to what you could bring in.

But you don't give a 14-year-old kid a bunch of diamonds and stuff, you know. She just look upon it as some glass. OK, you can have it, you know, that type of thing. [LAUGHS]

INT: Do you recall your last day home?

LM: Yes, because we went to the-- a train station. And Frankfurt had a big train station. It was an end station, so the trains would come in and back out again. It was called the sackbahnhof, a bag station. And my brother had come back from France by that time. And he, and Father, and Mother, and I, and this other girl met at-- were at the station.

And I don't remember where my brother was as the train pulled out, but I can still see my father standing there with his head down and bracing himself on his cane, not even looking up. And before I got on the train, he said to me, if you're not back by the time you're 16, we will never see each other anymore on this Earth.

And that turned out to be true. So I was not back by the time I was 16. And he died later that year. But you know, you say a lot of things. At that time, I didn't take it seriously. But still, I always remember my father standing there like that. And--

INT: Did you have a chance to say goodbye to Fredel, your best friend?

LM: Oh, I'm sure I did. I don't remember too much about it, because we were together in that youth group. There were-- there was a whole bunch of us that was very close. But I was the first one to leave. And-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] but she also was about the only one that I kept hearing from afterwards.

INT: Could you describe your journey to America?

LM: I wanted to die. [LAUGHS] The-- the ship started moving and I was seasick. [LAUGHS] The English Channel was terrible. It was in November. It was an unusually stormy crossing. And instead of seven days for the crossing, it took 11 days.

And finally, after a few days, a stewardess came to the cabin. This little girl from Frankfurt and I were in the cabin together. And we were in third class so we were in bunks. And I was on the top bunk, because she was a little girl, and I had long legs. But I never moved out of that bunk for days, it seemed like.

And the stewardess finally came. You've got to get up and get something to eat. And I said, no, I'm staying here. I'm going to die here. And she said, I'm going to bring you some oatmeal and a half of a grapefruit.

Well, oatmeal, I have always detested. I said, if you bring me that, I'll get sicker. And grapefruit, I had no idea what it was. And she said, well, I'll fix you up. Well, she came back with both of those items and made me eat it. And it stayed down. Of course, I was so empty.

And eventually, she helped me get up, and got dressed, and got some fresh air. And then I was able to stay. Well, the food stayed with me most of the time.

But it was so stormy on that crossing-- I don't know if they still do that. But at that time, they had ledges on the edges of the tables. And when the weather got bad, those edges were flipped up so that the cutlery, and china, and stuff would not slide off.

INT: Who was supervising you?

LM: I don't remember that anybody was. Like I say, a stewardess came to the cabin. There was nobody officially that I know of. I imagine that probably the captain knew that-- what our-- that our group was there. There were about 15 of us. And somehow, the purser and the stewards in the area where we were in the cabins kept an eye on us. It was very informal.

In fact, a few years later, this boy from Berlin that I mentioned before and I met in New York City. And the ship that we came over on was called the NewYork.It was a ship from the [? Hamburg ?] line. Was in harbor-- in the harbor. [? Gert ?] had found out about it.

He said, let's go down and take a look at the NewYork.And I said, OK, let's go. So we did. And sure enough, there were some of the personnel that remembered us. This was probably three years later or four years later, even. Yes, it was in '38-- no, '37. It was three years later.

And we just had a good time talking with those people in both German and English. Of course, by that time, the Hitler situation was worse in Germany. And I remember one of them saying to us, you kids were lucky to get out when you did. And he was on a German ship. [LAUGHS]

INT: Did you make friends on the ship?

LM: With this one boy, yes. We kept in touch. He was placed with a family in Newburgh on the Hudson. And I went up there once to visit him in the summertime, met his foster parents. And then when the war started in '39, he joined the British-- the Canadian Air Force. And then when the United States got into the war, he switched over to the American forces. And unfortunately, he was killed in Africa. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] So--

INT: So how did you entertain yourself on the ship?

LM: I guess we were just probably playing around. We were a bunch of kids. The oldest one was 15, probably. And the others were littler ones. And I don't know, maybe they had some personnel that kind of kept an eye on children. I really don't remember. It was 64 years ago.

INT: What was your-- where-- where did the boat dock?

LM: New York City.

INT: What was your initial reaction when you-- did you see the Statue of Liberty?

LM: Oh, yes. That was a thrill.

INT: What was your initial reaction?

LM: That was a thrill. It really was. It was good to see that-- not so much in contact of-- context and what it represented, but the mere fact that I'd heard about the Statue of Liberty. And I thought it was the end of the journey. I wasn't going to be seasick anymore. And I was going to get solid ground under my feet.

INT: And what was your first reaction to New York?

LM: Awfully busy, a lot of language I couldn't understand.

INT: Did you speak any English at all?

LM: None to-- none that you would call speaking. [LAUGHS]

INT: So where did you go? When you came to New York, what happened next?

LM: The family that was going to take me as a foster child had reneged while I was out in high seas. So the committee had to look for another family for myself. And it so happened that my uncle, one of my uncles, had two old-maid friends in New York City. And I was able to stay with them. That had been somehow arranged probably by telegram. [INAUDIBLE].INT: Tape number three. Survivor is Lotte Magnus. The date is December 11, 1998. Mrs. Magnus, we left off on the last tape-- we were talking about coming to New York and that the family that you were supposed to go to reneged. And you ended up going through an uncle, did I understand correctly? To another thing.

LM: An uncle some old-maid friends in New York City. And I stayed with them. I think it was probably about a week. They spoke German. So I was in good shape because in-- I took English in high school in Germany, in Frankfurt.

But there was a confusion about-- I was absent the first few weeks. And when I got to-- back to school, there were these funny-- signs on the board that the teacher had put up there. And I studied them.

And it turned out that they were phonics. She was teaching them pronunciation by the phonics. And I just assumed, well, English as a language, like Hebrew, you have a different kind of alphabet. So I studied that real hard.

And then after about a week or two, when I got-- after I got back, she switched to the regular alphabet. And I was just so confused that I said, I'll never want to go to a country where I have to speak English. And because of my experience with the teachers in Germany, I never wanted to teach school-- yeah, be a schoolteacher either. So well, you know, famous last words-- I've done both.

But anyhow, to get back to this other situation, in the meantime, the committee did find a couple in Pennsylvania who had two small boys of their own and had already taken a foster child, a boy from Nuremberg. And they said that they would take me temporarily.

So I then traveled to Abington, Pennsylvania, which is a suburb of Philadelphia. And I got there probably about the middle of December. Was just before Christmas vacation started. And they had me go to school right away.

This other boy, Peter, who had been with them for, I don't know how long, but a little bit, he had already been enrolled in school. So he took me up to school with him, and got me enrolled, and all that. At that time, all those things were very informal.

And so I was going to an American school. And sciences and math, I was always very good. But when it came to anything that needed English language, it was a different story.

And because of my lack of English, they put me back in English, and then civics, and history, and all those things, like any child that came in like I did. And in order to catch up a little bit, then, during the summer, I went to summer school, so that by the time school started again the following fall, I was a ninth grader.

INT: So--

LM: But in the meantime, you probably want to know about the family that I was placed with.

INT: I do.

LM: Well, he was-- they were both from Germany. He was a veteran of World War I and had come to this country in the middle '20s because of the inflation and everything in Germany. He came from a wealthy banker's family in the southern part of Germany and had established himself in commerce, in banks, here very nicely.

And she was a pediatric nurse from the Berlin area who became acquainted with him through his brother, who was a professor of psychology in Berlin where she was a nurse. And they eventually married.

And she came to this country-- a Jewish woman. And he was Jewish too. Originally, the family was Jewish. But they had joined the Catholic Church probably one or two generations before. But that, of course, didn't make any difference to Hitler.

But that was-- he did not leave because of that in the middle '20s, he left because of the poor economic prospects. And she then came out towards the end of the '20s. And at that time, things were economically no better.

And I'm sure that she had heard about the rumblings that Hitler was doing. And she wanted to get out. And they married and had, like I said, two small boys by the time I joined them in 1934. About-- at that time, they must have been about 2 and 4, something like that.

INT: What were their names?

LM: One was Peter, that was the older one. And Tommy was the younger one.

INT: And what was the family's last name?

LM: Klopfer-- K-L-O-P-F-E-R.

INT: And were you treated well?

LM: Most of the time.

INT: Were there any difficulties?

LM: Yes.

INT: What were some of the difficulties?

LM: There were. Well, he was not getting everything from his wife that he wanted. And he had girlfriends downtown too. She knew that. And it became very obvious to me very soon too. And I had to watch myself around him. And most of the time, I was successful, sometimes not.

And at that time, a girl in my position and in the situation that I was in, I thought long-- often about maybe letting the committee know. But then, it was going to be his word against my word. And things were not like they are now, where you can get more assistance and all that.

So I tried to keep my distance from him. And yet I had to play ball, to some extent and-- not that I had to be friendly. I couldn't be too antagonistic, because after all, it was my home. And most of the time, things were all right.

INT: Did you have contact with your parents at this time? Were you writing?

LM: Oh, yes, we corresponded all the time. In fact, one summer, also, Mrs. Klopfer took her two little boys to Germany to visit the grandparents. And they stopped in Frankfurt and met my parents. And my brother remembers them being there too. So--

INT: But you didn't alert your parents to what was going on with this man?

LM: No. The only thing I alerted my parents to was that one time I wrote that on that particular weekend, I had done the family laundry. But I didn't tell them that in America, you have washing machines. [LAUGHS] [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And I knew I'd get a reaction out of my mother, because in Germany, when the family laundry was done, that was always an accumulation of about four or five weeks. Because each level in the apartment house could use the laundry facilities only ever-- every few weeks.

And Mother wrote back, I didn't send you to the United States to become a washer woman. So I wrote back that-- that there are washing machines here, and you wash every week, and et cetera, et cetera. So-- and--

INT: So when this woman went-- took her children back to Germany and visited your parents, did--

LM: Her--

INT: Or her family, right?

LM: It was his family, really, that they visited. The grandparents of the children were from the father's side.

INT: Did the woman come back to report how anything had changed there to you? Did she tell you what was--

LM: Well, by that time, I had become aware, too, of what was going on in Germany. It was sometime before then that I had been here about a year. And I said something to the B'nai B'rith people about going back. And they said, we'd advise you not to. And they enlightened me some more.

And Bruno Klopfer, the brother in New York, as well as Hubert Klopfer, the one that was the head of the household where I was living, they always talked about the different things that-- they read the newspapers, and could understand the radio newscasts, and all that.

And I became more aware of it too. And of course, I was seeking these things out in the papers too. And I knew what was going on then and realized that it would be foolish to go back-- to want to go back, even.

INT: Did you tell your new friends in America why you had left Germany and what some of the problems were? Did you discuss it with them.

LM: Yes. They were all aware of the fact that I was a refugee from Germany. And this other boy, his name, also, was Peter-- the other foster child. He was in the same situation. We went to the same school. And they knew that we came from there. But they were not really aware of what was going on in Germany, not at all. A few Jewish families were more aware. But there were very few Jewish people around that area.

INT: What happened to the little girl that you were with?

LM: I don't know with what family she was placed somewhere. I have [INAUDIBLE] in-- oh, probably in the last 10 years or so, I once wrote to the Jewish Welfare or something in Philadelphia to see if they had any records of what became of some of the children that I came over with. I never even got an answer from the office, which was very disappointing.

INT: So how would you describe your adjustment to your high school? And--

LM: I think it was pretty good. It was difficult because of the language. And I had a number of-- quite a bit of household work to do too and-- at the Klopfers', which was perfectly all right. And it, in a way, was very good, because I really didn't have any friends at school. One girl, I was friendly with-- in fact, we're still in contact all the time. I owe her a letter right now.

But otherwise, I was never included in any of the parties or anything. I would go to social activities at school, but always by myself. And I can't remember ever even having had a date. And in Germany, I had had boyfriends.

And during the last two or three weeks of high school, just before graduation, one of the girls asked me once to come to a party that she was having for all the girls in our graduating class. But I didn't go. I figured, hey, you couldn't be bothered with me all these years, I can't be bothered with you now.

And I think-- I know that the members of my high school class became much more aware of my situation and what I had gone through later on. Because when we had our 50th high school reunion-- high school graduation reunion, I went back to Jenkintown for it.

And I was voted the outstanding classmate of the class of '38. I have a plaque hanging on the wall over there about it. And through this one girl, with whom I'm still in contact, she was the one that kept informing them what I was doing, that I-- and that's-- what was happening with my family, and with my life, and everything.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] so I was speechless when they handed me that. And one of the boys said, it's the first time I've heard you being speechless. [LAUGHS]

INT: Did you go on for higher education after that?

LM: Yes, I went to college in Colorado. There was a teacher's college, but with the-- the hope of being able to go on into dietetics internship from there.

The-- through the person that got me interested in that school, I found out that in a few years-- a few years previously, a student had them change or adapt the curriculum so that when she got her bachelor's degree in teaching, she had also achieved the requirements for the dietetics internship, which is a prerogative a year of internship in order to become a registered dietitian.

At that time, we weren't registered. And I could do that. And I also was assured that, in case my mother should be able to come to this country after I have had two years of schooling, which was just about right with the timing and everything, I could go out into rural schools to teach there on a limited certificate, and make enough to support the two of us, and then come back during the summers to take more summer school courses in order to get my bachelor's.

Well, it wasn't necessary for me to go out to teach. So I went ahead and finished my degree with an interruption of teaching, because I'd gotten such a good opportunity to teach between my junior and senior year in a girls' junior college in the Middle West, and then came back and finished in '43, and went into the internship.

INT: When did you learn of your father's death? When-- when did he die?

LM: He died in 1936. Mother had tried to commit suicide. And fortunately, my brother found her before it was too late and got her into the hospital. And my father could not be left alone at home because of his handicap. And at that time, our maid was no longer with us, because the Jew-- the Nazi laws had forbidden women in a certain age group to be working for Jewish families. And reluctantly, she had to leave.

So my father would have been home alone and with his handicap. He could not handle it. And my brother was not able to take care of him either. So our doctor put him in the hospital for the length of time it would take my mother to recuperate, if she could recuperate. It was a very touchy situation for a good many days.

And I guess, during that time, my father got so depressed and desperate that he slashed his wrists and killed himself. That was in November of 1936, which was about a half a year after I had had my 16th birthday-- and as he had predicted, that if I wasn't home by then, we would not see each other.

INT: And did you know why-- what were the circumstances that your mother tried to commit suicide?

LM: Because of the financial situation and because of my father's illness-- handicap. He had done some unethical things in the business and that she could not prevent. And she just got desperate what was going to happen to them. And this was the only way out that she could see.

But they were able to save her life. Father was buried while she was still in intensive care, I guess, and couldn't even go to the funeral. And I guess she didn't even know that Father had killed himself until quite a bit later.

INT: How were you notified of all of this?

LM: Well, of all the mail that I had been getting all these years, that was the only letter that was lost. And there were several weeks that I did not hear anything from home. I had no idea what was going on. At that time, you didn't telephone like you do now, of course.

And then one day, I came home from school at noontime. And Mrs. Klopfer-- I called her Mommy-- called me upstairs to her room. So she said, after you've finished eating, come upstairs for a minute, which wasn't unusual.

So I went up. And she had a letter there in front of her. She had opened the mail that I finally had gotten, because she also felt that something must be wrong. And she read in the letter what had happened.

INT: Well, who notified you, actually?

LM: My aunt, this young aunt. And she wrote-- she wrote in that letter that [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] your father is dead, your mother is not expected to live. But then, of course, Mother did pull through. And then I started to hear again fairly regularly.

And for some reason, the way-- I have the-- I did have the envelope for quite a while. It was just that the German handwriting was not read correctly. They read it as Franklintown instead of Jenkintown. And that's how come I didn't get that letter. So-- but that's just one of those things.

INT: And when did your brother actually-- did he stay in Germany? What happened to your brother?

LM: Yes, he had to stay in Germany. There was no place else for him to go after he came back from France. At first, he traveled for my father. And then when Father killed himself, the business had to be dissolved. There wasn't much left anyhow.

And then he got a job as a traveling salesman for a-- with a Jewish firm, a jewelry firm-- custom jewelry, primarily, which also sold to foreign countries, which was very desirable for the German government, because that brought foreign money into the country.

And it was because of that connection that he was able to get out of concentration camp in 1938 then, because his boss and-- let it be known to the authorities that his business was bringing foreign currency into the country. And they were so overwhelmed by all the Jews that they took in after the Crystal Night that they had to let some of the people go. So they let this man go.

And he said, I need Hans Goldschmidt, because he's my best salesman, which I guess he really was. And that's how Hans got out. He didn't go back on the road for the man, because he was busy, then, getting himself and his wife out of Germany.

INT: Did he tell you what concentration camp he was in?

LM: Oh, yes, he was in Buchenwald by Weimar. In fact, Hans knew pretty quickly where they were going, because he had traveled in that area. And he said, they were on the train from Frankfurt. Nobody told them where they were being taken. But he recognized the landscapes. And he knew that they were going to Weimar. And he had heard of the concentration camp Buchenwald. So he knew.

INT: And when did he-- did he ultimately come to America?

LM: Oh, yes. He-- well, I don't think I mentioned this before, but on Thanksgiving Day of 1938, I had a letter at Greeley-- I was going to college there then-- from my mother, in which she said that none of the men were at home, which to me indicated that they had been taken, which meant my brother and his father-in-law. I thought my grandfather was among them too, but I've since found out that my grandfather was never taken. He was too old, I guess, or too sick.

And anyhow, with that information, I could approach this Professor Weight. He and his wife were the ones that made it possible for me to go to college in Colorado. And he had always said, well, when the time is right, we'll give the affidavits for-- for your family.

Well, the time was never right. As far as they were concerned, it wasn't bad enough. But when I could confront them with that, he said, OK, [? Francis ?] and I-- that was his wife-- we'll give the-- the affidavits for your brother and his wife. We can only do it for two people. But there is a Jewish merchant in town. And we'll approach him and see what he can do for the others.

So he called him that Thanksgiving Day. And we went over to see him after dinner that evening. And I told him the story. He was a refugee-- well, not a refugee, but he had come from Poland around 1900.

And he said, yes, I'm financially in a position to give affidavits for the others. Then the day after Thanksgiving, the two men drove down to Denver from Greeley, Colorado-- it's about 80 miles-- and saw the Jewish Committee down there, and got the papers going.

Then by the time my brother was released from the concentration camp, the papers were there or came very quickly. And though the number was way too high for them to come to this country right away, they-- my sister-in-law had relatives in England.

And through them, they could come to England for a limited time, until their quota number would be ready. And they were in England for 13 months and then came over here in March of 1940.

INT: When did you meet your husband?

LM: My husband? On January 8 of 1948.

INT: And how did you meet?

LM: Through a girl that I went to school with in Frankfurt, whose mother was also a very good friend of my mother's. And when my mother came over to this country in 1946 after having been released from the concentration camp, she said, I need to see [? Ermgard ?] because I was present when her parents died. And this information that I can verify may help her to get restitution.

And so that's when I made contact with this [? Ermgard ?] again. She had gotten married by that time. I didn't even know that she was in New York, but Mother knew. And then she, and her husband, and I stayed in contact, especially after I got out of the service and was working in Philadelphia again after the war. And I'd go over and see them for the weekends.

And they were always looking for a man for me. They had this fabulous insurance salesman who was just right for you, Lotte, except he's a little short. But he was never in town when I was off work. [LAUGHS]

So one day, out of desperation, they said we've got to produce a man. And [? Ermgard ?] was a power machine operator. And one of the women that she had befriended there, also a refugee, had a brother who was unmarried, and tall, and a nice chap. So they invited him for that Friday evening. And that was Jim. [LAUGHS]

INT: And he was also a refugee?

LM: Yes, mm-hmm.

INT: And you mentioned a little bit earlier that you were in the service.

LM: Yes.

INT: What service? What--

LM: The United States Army Medical Corps as a dietitian-- hospital dietitian.

INT: And where were you stationed?

LM: And I took my basic training at Camp Carson in Colorado. I was stationed in-- at Fort Leonard Wood. And at Fort Leonard Wood, I took part of my dietetics internship, as well, under civil service. And then I was stationed in Honolulu for a short time, and Saipan, and a longer time in Guam. And I came back from Guam on emergency leave in November of '46, because Mother was coming over to the country.

Before I went in the service, even-- well, at the time that I accepted my commission, I let it be known that I hoped it would be-- would be possible for me to come to the States to meet my mother if she survives concentration camp and if she can come to this country. And I was assured all along the lines that that could be arranged. And of course, it was.

INT: Did you know that your mother was in a concentration camp?

LM: Yes.

INT: How did you know that?

LM: I don't really-- well, the thing was this-- Mother was on her way to this country in May of 1940. She was to sail from Holland. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It was either the 9th or the 10th of May. But the night before, Hitler overran Holland. And nobody got out anymore. So she was caught in Holland.

And she had been going out with a man in Frankfurt whom we all knew. His wife had died previously. And the-- Mother and this man, [? Arnold ?] [? Salomons, ?] had been dating a little bit in Holland. He was a Dutchman by birth. And he had gone back to Holland with his two children because of the Hitler situation. But of course, in the end, it was just as bad in Holland.

And he had already sent his son to-- I think it was Bolivia. They had some connection there. But his daughter, who was a little younger than I, was with him in Holland. And he had a sister living there.

So when Mother-- well, he knew that mother was going to come through Rotterdam in order to come to the States. And he made it his business to meet her when she was coming through. And they were together at the time that Hitler overran Rotterdam.

INT: What happened to your mother?

LM: What happened to my mother? Well, she was-- the first few nights, [? Arnold ?] put her up someplace. And then she was hidden by some family in Rotterdam for a while. But eventually, she was discovered and had to leave. Or they had to stop hiding somebody. And during thatt time, then, also, she and [? Arnold ?] got married. And--

